<p>YouTube shows a channel through four pictures. Each has a size, a safe area and a job, and each is a frame here.</p>
<ul>
	<li><a href="/content-creation/youtube/channel-art/">Channel art</a> — 2560 × 1440, of which every device shows only a band 423 tall, and a phone only the middle 1546 of it.</li>
	<li><a href="/content-creation/youtube/profile-art/">Profile art</a> — 800 × 800, shown as a circle at 40 pixels. Three: the illustration, the mark, a photograph.</li>
	<li><a href="/content-creation/youtube/thumbnails/">Thumbnails</a> — 1280 × 720, ten styles, and the grid they are judged in.</li>
	<li><a href="/content-creation/youtube/end-screen/">End screen</a> — 1920 × 1080, the last twenty seconds, with the slots where YouTube lays its tiles.</li>
	<li><a href="/content-creation/youtube/pixelmator/">Pixelmator templates</a> — every frame rebuilt as editable layers, by script.</li>
</ul>
<h2>As YouTube draws it</h2>
<p>The channel page, with the art cropped to its band, the profile beside the name, and four thumbnails in the grid. This is the only honest test of the set.</p>
